@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-error-taxonomy-immutable-clone-and-root-path-semantics
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch: error taxonomy, immutable clone, and root-path semantics"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` builds on `resolvePointer`. dl-001 already decided that pointer-resolution failures throw
+`JsonPointerError`, and that future features should reuse it for their own pointer-related failures
+rather than inventing a parallel type. JSON Patch (RFC 6902) also has failure modes that are not about
+pointer resolution: a malformed patch document, an unrecognised `op`, an operation missing a required
+member, or a `test` operation whose value does not match. The RFC is also silent or ambiguous on a few
+points that an implementation must still decide: how to guarantee the input document is never mutated,
+and what a zero-length (root) `path`/`from` means for each operation.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Add a second dedicated error type, `JsonPatchError` (exported from `src/index.ts`), for failures that
+  are about the patch itself rather than about resolving a pointer: an invalid patch document, an
+  unrecognised or missing `op`, an operation missing a required member (`path`, `from` for `move`/
+  `copy`, `value` for `add`/`replace`/`test`), and a failed `test` operation. A `path` or `from` that is
+  syntactically invalid, or that does not resolve where the operation requires it to, continues to throw
+  `JsonPointerError`, per dl-001. Consumers can therefore distinguish "this patch is malformed or
+  asserted something false" from "this pointer did not resolve" while still catching one type for each.
+- Guarantee immutability by cloning the whole input document with `structuredClone` once, up front, and
+  applying every operation to the clone. This is simpler and easier to verify than tracking which
+  branches of the structure an operation path touches, and `structuredClone` is a zero-dependency
+  built-in, consistent with the project's no-runtime-dependency constraint. Every operation (including
+  `copy`, which could otherwise alias a subtree into two locations) deep-clones the value it inserts.
+- Treat a zero-length `path`/`from` (the whole document) as a valid target for every operation, rather
+  than rejecting it: `add`/`replace`/`move`/`copy` with `path: ""` replace the whole document with the
+  given or moved/copied value; `test` with `path: ""` compares the whole document; `remove` with
+  `path: ""` sets the result to `undefined`. RFC 6902 explicitly allows this for `add`, and the other
+  operations are defined in terms of `add`/`remove`/a pointer resolution, so extending the same rule to
+  all six keeps their behaviour uniform instead of special-casing root away.
+- Implement `move` as a literal `remove` from `from` followed by an `add` at `path`, in that order, with
+  no extra check that `from` is not a prefix of `path`. Removing first means that if `path` is inside
+  the subtree being moved, the `add`'s parent lookup naturally fails with `JsonPointerError` — the RFC's
+  prohibition on moving a value into one of its own children falls out of sequencing rather than needing
+  its own check.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Catching `JsonPointerError` alone is not enough to catch every `applyPatch` failure; a caller that
+  wants to catch "anything went wrong" must catch both `JsonPatchError` and `JsonPointerError` (or
+  `Error`).
+- `structuredClone` makes the mutation guarantee easy to verify but clones the entire document for every
+  call, even a single-operation patch touching one key; this is the right trade-off for a correctness-
+  first toolkit with no stated performance requirement, and can be revisited if profiling ever shows
+  otherwise.
+- `remove` with `path: ""` returns `undefined` rather than throwing; a future caller that needs "the
+  document must remain a JSON value" can special-case that before calling `applyPatch`, since RFC 6902
+  does not itself forbid emptying the document this way.
