@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902)"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902), with its six operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`,
+`test`), and export `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It must return a new document
+without mutating its input, and throw when the patch or one of its operations is invalid.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Added `applyPatch(document, patch)`, a `JsonPatchError` class, and the `JsonPatchOperation` type to
+  `src/index.ts`, building on the existing `resolvePointer`/`JsonPointerError`/`step` machinery.
+- Refactored `resolvePointer`'s inline pointer parsing into a shared `parsePointer` helper, reused by
+  both `resolvePointer` and the patch operations.
+- `applyPatch` clones `document` with `structuredClone` up front and applies every operation to the
+  clone, so the input is never mutated even when a later operation throws.
+- Each operation is validated structurally (`op` is one of the six, `path` is a string, `from` is
+  required for `move`/`copy`, `value` is required for `add`/`replace`/`test`) before being applied;
+  structural problems and failed `test` assertions throw `JsonPatchError`. An invalid or non-resolving
+  `path`/`from` throws `JsonPointerError`, per dl-001.
+- `add` supports the `-` array index (append) and an index equal to the array's length (insert at the
+  end); `remove`/`replace` require the target to already exist. `move` is implemented as a `remove`
+  from `from` followed by an `add` at `path`, which naturally rejects moving a value into one of its own
+  children once the parent lookup for `path` fails. `copy` deep-clones the value it duplicates so the
+  two locations never alias.
+- A zero-length `path`/`from` targets the whole document for every operation (see dl-002 for the
+  reasoning, including why `remove` with `path: ""` yields `undefined`).
+- Wrote `src/index.test.ts` first (red/green): the RFC 6902 appendix A worked examples (skipping A.13,
+  which relies on duplicate JSON object keys and has no analogue for an API that takes a JS object),
+  plus coverage for root-path operations, `copy` non-aliasing, immutability (including when an
+  operation fails partway through a multi-operation patch), and one error case per validation rule.
+- Documented `applyPatch` in `README.md`.
+- Recorded dl-002 for the error-taxonomy, immutable-clone, and root-path design decisions.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test`: 38/38 passing.
+- `npm run typecheck`: no errors.
